WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:02.140
Hi, welcome back to the Lam Rim,

00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:05.310
and we're going to do a review

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.550
of all the points that we've covered

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:10.340
in this Lam Rim course together.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:12.946
I think it's 37, Tim or 38?

00:00:12.946 --> 00:00:14.480
37, I guess.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.457
Anyway, it's something like that.

00:00:16.457 --> 00:00:18.224
[Tim] It's 38. [GMR] 38.

00:00:18.224 --> 00:00:22.223
And we're going to go
through... I picked out,

00:00:22.223 --> 00:00:27.185
there's about 80 cool ideas
that we have pictures for,

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:32.196
and I picked out about 16.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:37.890
And this is the purpose of
choosing these photographs,

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.810
is to make it easy for all of us

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:43.980
to review the big ideas
that we went through.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.756
It's kind of like a meditation.

00:00:46.756 --> 00:00:49.530
So if you're going to do a meditation

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.700
on this whole course,

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:55.310
you can go through the pictures one by one

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:58.838
and you can test yourself,
and it's kind of fun.

00:00:58.838 --> 00:01:01.220
I finish writing the course

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:03.840
then I go through the
photographs and honestly,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.570
I cannot remember what each one is.

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:09.117
Maybe 90% and then I...

00:01:09.117 --> 00:01:12.750
a couple of them I don't
remember why I chose the picture.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.400
And then I remind myself,
and then I won't forget it.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:19.180
So I think it's a wonderful way to

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:24.180
meditate on the main points
of this whole course.

00:01:24.545 --> 00:01:27.790
Let's do a self examination.

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:32.300
Let's look at the 16
most important pictures.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:36.180
And my friends in this very room

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:40.800
are going to tell me
what's the picture about.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.770
So it's a great teaching technique

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:46.130
is to embarrass your friends.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:49.130
And then they'd never forget

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:53.670
that they embarrassed themselves
in front of so many people.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:56.967
This is why we debate in the monastery.

00:01:56.967 --> 00:02:00.200
The debates I lost I remember deeply.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:01.980
The debates I won, I can't remember.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.840
So, all right, here we go.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.403
First picture.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:11.530
Do you guys remember?
I'll give you a clue.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.410
This had to do with the virus.

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:14.723
Does anybody?

00:02:14.723 --> 00:02:16.470
[Student] Sending the virus
to the middle of the sun.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:20.220
[GMR] Yeah good, sending the
virus to the middle of the sun.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:23.620
And I don't remember the
temperature difference

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:26.200
but it's amazing. It's incredible.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:29.300
The temperature is incredible
in the middle of the sun.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:32.710
And the idea is that bodhicitta,

00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:36.140
or the vision of the
bodhisattva jumping from

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:39.980
planet to planet, billions
of planets in a few seconds

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.260
and meeting each person
there and taking care

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.470
of each person there.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:47.410
The decision to take care
of each person there,

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:49.520
that's so powerful

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.520
that something like COVID is
not going to stand up to it.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:03:00.210
If any group in the world
does some serious study

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:04.310
about bodhicitta, we can
easily kill the virus.

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:07.440
It's like sending it to
the middle of the sun.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.470
So that's the first picture
I want you guys to remember,

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:14.960
is the power of this decision

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.840
to take care of all the
people in the universe.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.323
All right, second picture, Mr. Pachi.

00:03:22.323 --> 00:03:27.323
You guys remember this LAG MTHIL DU GDAMS?

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:29.978
What's it mean?

00:03:29.978 --> 00:03:32.908
(unclear)

00:03:32.908 --> 00:03:36.298
Yes. The name of this book

00:03:36.298 --> 00:03:41.298
is A Gift of Liberation Thrust
Into The Palm of Your Hand.

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:45.770
But there's a different palm
of your hand in scripture

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:50.040
which means to keep something as your

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.820
primary practice, this
is my main practice.

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:55.560
Somebody says, "What are
you working on nowadays?"

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:57.160
- Oh, I'm working on
my number one practice.

00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:58.520
- What's that?

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:01.990
- I'm trying to get this
vision of a bodhisattva.

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:03.900
So bodhicitta,

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:08.090
which I like to call
vision of a bodhisattva,

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:10.490
that's the main thing I'm focusing on.

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:12.550
That's my main practice I'm focusing on.

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:15.890
So he says, "Put that in
the palm of your hand,"

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:18.370
means keep it,

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:21.860
the world, all the people in
the world and the universe.

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:23.640
That should be your main practice,

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:26.130
and then you're on the right track.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:31.130
All right, third picture, Mr. Pachi.

00:04:32.096 --> 00:04:35.996
Yes I love this picture.
You should get your own.

00:04:35.996 --> 00:04:39.620
Find a picture of you with your mom

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:43.030
and keep it for this course.

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:46.160
Find a nice picture of you with your mom.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.710
And it's very important,

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:51.170
during this entire course,

00:04:51.170 --> 00:04:54.030
that you keep coming back to

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:57.070
this one person in your
life who took care of you.

00:04:57.070 --> 00:04:59.660
It's very important to
keep coming back to her.

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:02.490
And I'll tell you the first
time I saw the picture recently,

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:04.950
I don't look at it all the time,

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:09.610
I was thinking, oh yeah, that's my mom.

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:13.550
But then if you keep looking
at the picture every day,

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:16.810
then I think you cannot help

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:20.800
but go back and realize how much

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:24.200
hardship and love she put
into taking care of you.

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:26.771
And my mom had a lot of hardships,

00:05:26.771 --> 00:05:31.771
four sons, no husband
and very, very difficult.

00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:35.130
So I think

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:37.850
that's probably the most important picture

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:41.470
for this seven step practice

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:44.330
for reaching a bodhisattva state of mind,

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:47.070
is to keep a picture of your mom

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:51.330
close by and think about it
during this course a lot.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:54.800
And you'll never forget
that the goal of this course

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.470
is to realize that every
single person around you

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:01.490
has done the same thing for you.

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:06.479
All right, next one.

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:11.930
Wait a minute, that's the wrong picture,

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:14.110
you guys sure that's the next one?

00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:15.890
I'm thinking Dharmakirti.

00:06:15.890 --> 00:06:18.780
Oh no, you're right, you're
right, that's the right one.

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:20.540
Okay, I'm sorry.

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:23.050
Yeah, this was the proof of Dharmakirti.

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:25.780
I was expecting that
picture of Dharmakirti

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:28.762
but this'll do.

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:34.260
And we took a detour

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:37.960
because Pabongka Rinpoche
quoted Dharmakirti,

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:41.180
and I think he wanted us to take a detour

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:44.050
because he cut the
quotation in the middle.

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:46.250
And when it's cut in half,

00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:48.470
the quotation means the opposite

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:50.330
of what it's supposed to mean.

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:55.330
So, there's a 'not' at
the end that he left out.

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:58.970
And so, it's not the case

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:03.773
that the DBANG PO,

00:07:03.773 --> 00:07:06.200
the clarity of the senses of the child,

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:09.550
the life of the child

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:13.015
and the G-YER BAG CAN,

00:07:13.015 --> 00:07:15.563
the personality.

00:07:15.563 --> 00:07:17.160
Pau gave me this word,

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:20.870
the unique personality
of Maitri, of this baby,

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:23.450
he has his own personality.

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:27.340
And there can be twins
born at the same time.

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:30.000
Psychologists have trouble with that

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.751
because they want to say
their mom ate a milkshake.

00:07:34.751 --> 00:07:37.830
Their mom ate a really hot chile relleno

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:42.600
in the third month, and that's
why the kid is so angry.

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:44.930
But they have trouble with twins.

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:47.490
Then they have to say, the
chile relleno went to one twin

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:49.950
but the chile relleno
didn't go to the other twin.

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:53.550
And it gets crazy.

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:58.550
Yes, kids enter this life
with a certain personality,

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:04.400
and their sense powers display life.

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:06.080
They have life.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:10.050
The personality is not
coming from their skin.

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:13.020
Their personality is not
coming from chemicals.

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:15.530
Because if it was coming from chemicals,

00:08:15.530 --> 00:08:17.810
then every time you put
those chemicals in a bottle

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:22.210
you should have Maitri, but you don't.

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:24.360
What children are

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:26.760
is not just the result of chemicals.

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:28.970
Of course, it affects children,

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:31.520
but who they are

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:35.230
is flowing from a similar
cause in the past.

00:08:35.230 --> 00:08:37.071
Mind

00:08:37.071 --> 00:08:39.140
can only be the result of mind.

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:40.660
Which came first,

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:42.170
the chicken or the egg?

00:08:42.170 --> 00:08:44.346
Yes.

00:08:47.492 --> 00:08:48.914
Yes, every egg needs a chicken

00:08:48.914 --> 00:08:50.405
and every chicken needs an egg.

00:08:50.405 --> 00:08:55.388
Yes, therefore, if you can just struggle

00:08:55.388 --> 00:09:00.388
to prove that you were alive 10 minutes

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:03.967
before your mom and dad had sex

00:09:03.967 --> 00:09:07.710
and the egg was fertilized by the sperm.

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:12.090
If you can just force
your mind 10 minutes back

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:13.730
from that moment,

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:16.710
you have to say you were alive,

00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:18.350
something was alive,

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:19.680
something was aware,

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:20.910
you have to say.

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:25.440
If you can push back that 10 minutes,

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:27.490
then everyone's been your mother.

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:28.870
Then it's totally logical.

00:09:28.870 --> 00:09:31.360
Then you're already trapped.

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:33.668
Checkmate.

00:09:33.668 --> 00:09:35.600
Did you live 10 minutes before?

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:38.030
Yeah. Okay, then everyone's been your mom.

00:09:38.030 --> 00:09:39.738
Proof.

00:09:39.738 --> 00:09:42.650
It doesn't take a genius.

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:46.126
All right, next one, you guys.

00:09:47.643 --> 00:09:48.860
Do you remember this one?

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:53.860
It was an emphasis on
location. Do you remember?

00:09:54.035 --> 00:09:57.963
(students talking)

00:09:57.963 --> 00:10:01.620
[GMR] Yes. If you've had countless births,

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:04.660
then you have stepped on

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:09.635
or you have been born in every
square meter of the universe.

00:10:11.030 --> 00:10:14.100
Countless times, billions of times.

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:17.100
So it's kind of fun, I like
to walk out in the desert,

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.070
find a rock.

00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:22.050
It's so difficult at Diamond
Mountain to find a rock.

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:25.270
You just walk out and you find one rock

00:10:25.270 --> 00:10:28.067
and you say,

00:10:28.067 --> 00:10:31.113
"I have been born next to this rock

00:10:31.113 --> 00:10:33.010
billions and billions of times".

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:37.180
But it's not a single rock
out in the desert somewhere.

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:39.540
I have been born

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:42.850
at this rock, near this rock

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:45.770
billions of times.

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:49.129
And then you get a feeling
for how many times it's been.

00:10:49.129 --> 00:10:51.320
It's very difficult.

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:55.110
It's easy to throw around
the word countless.

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:57.090
We get lazy in Buddhism.

00:10:57.090 --> 00:10:59.560
How many lives you live? Countless lives.

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:02.100
Yes, but what does that really mean?

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:05.570
That means you've been born everywhere

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:07.460
billions of times.

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:10.590
Every country, every language,
every world, every planet,

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:12.250
every star,

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:13.250
billions of times.

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:15.325
Try to think like that.

00:11:15.325 --> 00:11:16.830
I like to go to Diamond Mountain,

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:18.360
go out

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:21.880
in a black moon, Yamantaka day,

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:24.620
and look at the sky,
you see the Milky Way.

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:27.878
This morning I was
listening to Stevie Wonder,

00:11:27.878 --> 00:11:30.267
"Ribbon in the Sky" and I was like,

00:11:30.267 --> 00:11:31.780
"Veronica, what's the Ribbon in the Sky?

00:11:31.780 --> 00:11:34.570
Maybe it's the Milky Way."

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:37.130
And billions and billions of stars.

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:41.040
I remember a Chinese lady from Shanghai.

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:43.330
I went to Shanghai. I taught in Shanghai.

00:11:43.330 --> 00:11:46.845
They told me to stay inside.

00:11:46.845 --> 00:11:48.710
Stay in the hotel, don't go outside.

00:11:48.710 --> 00:11:49.606
I'm like, "Why?"

00:11:49.606 --> 00:11:52.170
And they said, "Look out
the hotel right now".

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:53.620
And I looked to the hotel,

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:56.950
there's another part of the
hotel about 50 feet away.

00:11:56.950 --> 00:11:58.928
You couldn't see it.

00:11:58.928 --> 00:12:00.500
The pollution was so bad.

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:03.060
And they said, "Don't go outside."

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:08.060
I'm like, "Wow, that's weird."

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:11.025
You can go outside.

00:12:11.025 --> 00:12:14.057
And then a lady from China told me,

00:12:14.057 --> 00:12:16.040
"Hey, guess what?

00:12:16.040 --> 00:12:17.320
I came to Diamond Mountain.

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:19.870
I saw the Milky Way. I saw all the stars.

00:12:19.870 --> 00:12:21.300
Can I ask you a question?"

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:22.133
- Yes. What?

00:12:22.133 --> 00:12:26.090
- Will these stars be out tomorrow

00:12:26.090 --> 00:12:28.530
or is it just tonight?

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:30.780
Do they also have these stars tomorrow?

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:33.220
I'm like, "We have them every night."

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:34.640
She's like, "Wow!

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:38.640
I thought it was just
once a year or something."

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:41.430
Then look at those billions of stars

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:43.950
and think about,

00:12:43.950 --> 00:12:45.980
you've been on each one billions of times.

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:48.110
I also like to play a game with myself.

00:12:48.110 --> 00:12:50.790
I go out, there's an app, what is that?

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:53.082
Sky App or something?

00:12:53.082 --> 00:12:55.260
[Students] Sky Guide
or something like that.

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:56.610
[GMR] What is it called?

00:12:56.610 --> 00:12:58.080
Sky Guide.

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:00.050
Get it on your phone, it's so amazing.

00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:02.723
And it tells you

00:13:02.723 --> 00:13:04.940
what's the name of the star.

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:09.240
I like to play with my Sky Guide

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:13.140
and check out the next
star I'd like to be on

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:14.340
next life.

00:13:14.340 --> 00:13:17.895
I say where am I going next?

00:13:17.895 --> 00:13:19.930
It's very cool.

00:13:19.930 --> 00:13:22.654
All right.

00:13:22.654 --> 00:13:24.410
No place we haven't been born.

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:27.019
Next picture, you guys.

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:34.003
Do you remember what this story is?

00:13:34.003 --> 00:13:34.960
Come on.

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:38.656
I'll give you a clue, a wolf was involved.

00:13:38.656 --> 00:13:40.784
[Student] The story of

00:13:40.784 --> 00:13:43.471
the lady who suffers

00:13:43.471 --> 00:13:45.971
[GMR] Blue Lotus, Utpala.

00:13:45.971 --> 00:13:48.160
Utpala Varman,

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:50.998
Blue Colored Lotus.

00:13:52.381 --> 00:13:55.130
And this terrible, terrible story

00:13:55.130 --> 00:13:58.090
about this incredible nun

00:13:58.090 --> 00:14:00.440
who became an arhat

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:03.050
after she was personally greeted

00:14:03.050 --> 00:14:05.510
by the Buddha who stood up

00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:08.060
when she entered the garden

00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:10.820
and invited her to become a nun.

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:14.610
After she had all her children die,

00:14:14.610 --> 00:14:16.860
her parents die, her husbands die,

00:14:16.860 --> 00:14:20.472
and after she was buried
alive twice or three times.

00:14:23.137 --> 00:14:26.487
What's the point of this story?

00:14:26.487 --> 00:14:31.487
Why did they have this story in the book?

00:14:31.807 --> 00:14:34.870
[Student] To understand your past life.

00:14:34.870 --> 00:14:38.130
[GMR] Yes, there will come a day

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:39.900
when you will see your past life.

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:42.000
There will come a day

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:45.650
when you can see that everyone's
been your mom directly.

00:14:45.650 --> 00:14:47.760
So right now, you have
to do it with logic.

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:50.068
But there will come a day

00:14:50.068 --> 00:14:52.030
when you just look around
and you think, "Oh my god,

00:14:52.030 --> 00:14:53.480
they all have been my mom.

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:56.040
I can remember what they did.

00:14:56.040 --> 00:14:59.210
I remember my 10th birthday with her.

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:01.720
And I remember my sixth birthday with her.

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:05.553
And I remember he kicked
me when I was a kid."

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:10.070
And you can see all your past moms.

00:15:10.070 --> 00:15:14.514
So the story was very important.

00:15:14.514 --> 00:15:18.428
There was an argument that...

00:15:18.428 --> 00:15:21.800
don't think that just because
you cannot recognize right now

00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:23.560
that someone has been your mother before

00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:28.000
that they have never
been your mother because

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:32.350
the passage of life and death

00:15:32.350 --> 00:15:33.880
covers,

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:36.070
it obscures

00:15:36.070 --> 00:15:40.451
the reality that everyone has
been our mom countless times.

00:15:40.451 --> 00:15:44.840
You've died so many
times, you've forgotten.

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:47.700
Next picture.

00:15:47.700 --> 00:15:51.403
This one's tough!

00:15:51.403 --> 00:15:54.910
If you want to win the $50,

00:15:54.910 --> 00:15:59.880
you have to relate it to
another piece of clothing.

00:16:01.402 --> 00:16:02.940
[Student] It's related
to the monk's shawl.

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:03.870
[GMR] Yes, great.

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:05.300
It was related to the monk's shawl

00:16:05.300 --> 00:16:08.770
and Pabongka was making an argument.

00:16:08.770 --> 00:16:12.205
[Student] Dirty shawl, wasn't it?

00:16:12.205 --> 00:16:15.770
[GMR] Just because my
shawl has holes in it,

00:16:15.770 --> 00:16:17.845
doesn't prove...

00:16:17.845 --> 00:16:19.350
[Student] That it wasn't
whole to begin with.

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:21.590
[GMR] Yes, that it's not
the same shawl I bought

00:16:21.590 --> 00:16:24.320
in the market last year.

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:28.859
Yes, it looks different,
it's a little worse for wear.

00:16:28.859 --> 00:16:31.418
In English, we have this idiom,

00:16:31.418 --> 00:16:32.900
worse for wear.

00:16:32.900 --> 00:16:34.780
It means look, yeah, my shawl looks like

00:16:34.780 --> 00:16:37.110
it's been beaten up 100 times.

00:16:37.110 --> 00:16:40.700
That's kind of a funny analogy.

00:16:40.700 --> 00:16:42.760
Yes, you're going to run into
people that look like them

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:44.980
and beaten up by live 100 times, but still

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:48.390
they were your fresh, beautiful mom.

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:50.790
They're not different from
the fresh, beautiful mom

00:16:50.790 --> 00:16:53.090
in Geshe-la's picture

00:16:53.090 --> 00:16:56.005
when he was a little kid.

00:16:56.005 --> 00:17:00.170
Just because my monk shawl has holes in it

00:17:00.170 --> 00:17:02.360
doesn't prove it's not the same shawl

00:17:02.360 --> 00:17:04.660
I bought at the market last year.

00:17:04.660 --> 00:17:07.870
Just because the mother
sentient beings around you

00:17:07.870 --> 00:17:09.610
look a little worn out,

00:17:09.610 --> 00:17:11.760
doesn't mean they weren't that fresh mom

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:14.310
who gave you birth last life.

00:17:14.310 --> 00:17:16.020
It's the same mom,

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:20.381
it's just got holes in it.

00:17:20.381 --> 00:17:22.530
But just because it has holes doesn't mean

00:17:22.530 --> 00:17:25.240
it's not the same mom.

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:28.823
Next picture.

00:17:28.823 --> 00:17:31.150
What is this picture?

00:17:31.150 --> 00:17:33.842
What's the event?

00:17:33.842 --> 00:17:37.270
[Student] It's a Japanese
dancing in the Lam Rim.

00:17:37.270 --> 00:17:42.270
[GMR] Yes, it's the Lam
Rim event in Bali, 2019.

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:47.738
Why are we showing it here?

00:17:50.450 --> 00:17:52.220
I'll give you the first part of the logic,

00:17:52.220 --> 00:17:54.020
it's a logical statement.

00:17:54.020 --> 00:17:57.720
If your mom had not been so
skillful at saving your ass

00:17:57.720 --> 00:18:00.580
1,000 times every day,

00:18:00.580 --> 00:18:04.319
you wouldn't be sitting here
and listening to Lam Rim.

00:18:04.319 --> 00:18:06.340
What's the best thing
your mom ever did for you?

00:18:06.340 --> 00:18:09.108
Feed you baby food,

00:18:09.108 --> 00:18:10.880
give you milk,

00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:12.920
save you from falling off the table.

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:16.048
No, she got you here.

00:18:16.048 --> 00:18:17.760
So, maybe she's nice.

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:19.070
Maybe she's a nice person.

00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:21.430
She got you into the Lam Rim

00:18:21.430 --> 00:18:23.380
and that's the best thing
she ever did for you.

00:18:23.380 --> 00:18:27.230
I like this picture of the Lam
Rim because it's so joyful.

00:18:27.230 --> 00:18:28.610
And she got you there.

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:29.980
Those of you who were there,

00:18:29.980 --> 00:18:32.802
it was a very beautiful moment

00:18:32.802 --> 00:18:37.057
to see all of our friends
doing their traditional dances

00:18:37.057 --> 00:18:38.270
from many countries.

00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:40.740
And your mom got you here.

00:18:40.740 --> 00:18:43.510
That's the best thing she ever did.

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:45.260
Next one.

00:18:45.260 --> 00:18:47.550
Now, next one, somebody has
to tell me the whole story.

00:18:47.550 --> 00:18:49.640
I don't want to tell the story.

00:18:49.640 --> 00:18:52.190
Who's going to do it? Pachi?

00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:53.350
Loud so they can hear it.

00:18:53.350 --> 00:18:56.573
[Pachi] This is a story
of Mongolian children

00:18:56.573 --> 00:18:59.750
with no parents, wild children.

00:18:59.750 --> 00:19:01.670
Don't give for granted.

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:06.277
And they live in the sewer.

00:19:06.277 --> 00:19:10.910
If your mom had not worked so hard,

00:19:10.910 --> 00:19:14.052
you would be a wild animal.

00:19:14.052 --> 00:19:17.290
You would just be a wild animal.

00:19:17.290 --> 00:19:21.070
It's not that you're naturally
civilized, you're not.

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:23.130
Your mom civilizes you.

00:19:23.130 --> 00:19:27.460
So, take away the mom, like
the kids we saw in Mongolia,

00:19:27.460 --> 00:19:29.180
who are living in the sewer.

00:19:29.180 --> 00:19:31.130
They cannot speak human language.

00:19:31.130 --> 00:19:33.810
They don't understand human language.

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:37.983
They run around naked
and they bite tourists

00:19:37.983 --> 00:19:41.645
who don't give them their purse.

00:19:41.645 --> 00:19:43.830
You would be the same.
You would be the same.

00:19:43.830 --> 00:19:45.450
Without your mom,

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:49.116
you would be a wild animal.

00:19:49.116 --> 00:19:50.450
And you would be the same.

00:19:50.450 --> 00:19:53.216
So think about it.

00:19:53.216 --> 00:19:57.060
The difference between you
and a wild animal is your mom.

00:19:57.060 --> 00:19:59.150
And I think especially

00:19:59.150 --> 00:20:01.460
giving us the gift of speech,

00:20:01.460 --> 00:20:04.518
teaching us to speak.

00:20:04.518 --> 00:20:07.660
We don't always use it
for the best purpose but

00:20:07.660 --> 00:20:09.700
that's an extraordinary gift.

00:20:09.700 --> 00:20:13.650
All right, next one.

00:20:13.650 --> 00:20:17.158
Somebody tell me the whole story.

00:20:18.200 --> 00:20:19.802
For $0.

00:20:19.802 --> 00:20:23.336
[Student] If your mom is hitting you

00:20:23.336 --> 00:20:25.744
it's like on drug.

00:20:25.744 --> 00:20:28.442
[GMR] Yes, your mom
accidentally took some extra...

00:20:28.442 --> 00:20:30.117
[Student] Drugs. [GMR] Drugs.

00:20:30.117 --> 00:20:32.510
[Student] And she just
got a little bit violent,

00:20:32.510 --> 00:20:35.353
she doesn't recognize
you when you're coming

00:20:35.353 --> 00:20:38.070
to the room.

00:20:38.070 --> 00:20:41.120
You won't shoot her.

00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:46.120
You will try to just make her...

00:20:46.660 --> 00:20:48.351
[GMR] Peaceful.

00:20:48.351 --> 00:20:50.890
So this is the answer

00:20:50.890 --> 00:20:55.890
to the question that
people give to Buddhists.

00:20:56.040 --> 00:20:58.874
- Are you ahinsa? Do you have ahinsa?

00:20:58.874 --> 00:21:01.270
- Are you non-violent? - Yes.

00:21:01.270 --> 00:21:03.840
So if somebody comes at you with a knife

00:21:03.840 --> 00:21:06.840
and you have a gun, you won't shoot them?

00:21:06.840 --> 00:21:07.950
And you're like, "No."

00:21:07.950 --> 00:21:09.600
And they say, "Why?"

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:10.980
Well would you shoot your own mother

00:21:10.980 --> 00:21:12.350
if she came out at you with a knife?

00:21:12.350 --> 00:21:15.210
My mother's not going to come
at me with a knife, come on.

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:17.980
Yes, but if your mother
went temporarily crazy

00:21:17.980 --> 00:21:20.770
because she took some drugs by accident,

00:21:20.770 --> 00:21:24.570
and she was crazy and she
thought you're a thief,

00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:27.790
and she came out at you
with her kitchen knife,

00:21:27.790 --> 00:21:29.260
and she's about to kill you,

00:21:29.260 --> 00:21:31.060
would you shoot her then?

00:21:31.060 --> 00:21:33.893
And you're like, "Hmm."

00:21:33.893 --> 00:21:36.580
"You know, I wouldn't shoot
her, I would just grab her

00:21:36.580 --> 00:21:38.240
and try to knock her down

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:40.660
and take the knife out of her hand."

00:21:40.660 --> 00:21:42.430
Well, would you do that if you knew

00:21:42.430 --> 00:21:44.580
she was going to cut you badly?

00:21:44.580 --> 00:21:47.740
And then if you're a
good kid, you say, "Yes,

00:21:47.740 --> 00:21:50.560
I would allow her to cut me badly

00:21:50.560 --> 00:21:52.380
rather than shoot her."

00:21:52.380 --> 00:21:55.283
So that's the idea, is that

00:21:55.283 --> 00:21:58.290
people around us have temporary insanity.

00:21:58.290 --> 00:22:02.120
They do stupid, mean things to us,

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:04.470
but it doesn't mean that
that gives you the right

00:22:04.470 --> 00:22:05.880
to hurt them

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:07.210
because they are your mom

00:22:07.210 --> 00:22:08.580
and you wouldn't do it to your mom.

00:22:08.580 --> 00:22:10.150
If you're a good kid,

00:22:10.150 --> 00:22:12.320
you would not shoot a gun at your mom,

00:22:12.320 --> 00:22:14.740
even if she's trying to kill you.

00:22:14.740 --> 00:22:17.070
You would find a way to try to stop her.

00:22:17.070 --> 00:22:19.810
And this applies to countries
that attack your country

00:22:19.810 --> 00:22:21.920
and things like that.

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:25.728
Other countries which are
threatening your country.

00:22:25.728 --> 00:22:27.923
Is there a way

00:22:27.923 --> 00:22:30.990
that you could slow them down or stop them

00:22:30.990 --> 00:22:33.510
without hurting them?

00:22:33.510 --> 00:22:35.535
And then

00:22:35.535 --> 00:22:38.400
when we negotiate with other countries

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:42.046
and we have international problems,

00:22:42.046 --> 00:22:45.010
we have to remember
they have been our mom.

00:22:45.010 --> 00:22:48.241
All right, next one.

00:22:49.900 --> 00:22:54.092
Who wants to tell me what this is?

00:22:55.560 --> 00:23:00.560
- Six perfections. -Nice! It's
the six, wait, who wrote this?

00:23:00.650 --> 00:23:02.838
I'll give you a clue,
his picture is there.

00:23:02.838 --> 00:23:05.830
[Student] This is Arya Nagarjuna.

00:23:05.830 --> 00:23:07.110
[GMR] Nagarjuna wrote this

00:23:07.110 --> 00:23:09.257
and he went through the six
perfections and he said,

00:23:09.257 --> 00:23:12.088
"Here's the

00:23:16.080 --> 00:23:17.560
correlations."

00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:19.010
Here's the six correlations.

00:23:19.010 --> 00:23:20.150
You want to make money?

00:23:20.150 --> 00:23:21.660
Can we keep it up?

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:24.039
Sorry, Pachi.

00:23:25.800 --> 00:23:29.883
If you are generous to
people, you will become rich.

00:23:29.883 --> 00:23:31.730
If you don't hurt other people,

00:23:31.730 --> 00:23:33.360
you'll always be happy.

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:35.180
If you want to be beautiful,

00:23:35.180 --> 00:23:38.356
don't get upset at people.

00:23:38.356 --> 00:23:41.003
If you

00:23:41.003 --> 00:23:44.930
are careful to be joyful
when you do your dharma,

00:23:44.930 --> 00:23:48.930
then actually people will
recognize you as a leader.

00:23:48.930 --> 00:23:52.420
People will accept you as a leader.

00:23:52.420 --> 00:23:54.840
And if you meditate regularly,

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:56.880
even lousy meditation,

00:23:56.880 --> 00:23:59.010
you will be peaceful.

00:23:59.010 --> 00:24:02.540
Because it's karma. It's not
the success of the meditation,

00:24:02.540 --> 00:24:05.548
it's the fact that you tried to meditate.

00:24:05.548 --> 00:24:08.440
And if you try to remember
that things are coming

00:24:08.440 --> 00:24:10.080
from your seeds,

00:24:10.080 --> 00:24:13.320
then eventually you'll
be freed from suffering.

00:24:13.320 --> 00:24:16.580
Now, I think Christina,

00:24:16.580 --> 00:24:21.400
why did I stick this in
this part of the Lam Rim?

00:24:21.400 --> 00:24:22.730
Pabongka did not put this

00:24:22.730 --> 00:24:24.380
in this part of the Lam Rim.

00:24:24.380 --> 00:24:26.344
But he should have.

00:24:26.344 --> 00:24:27.788
[Christina] Was it because Chandrakirti

00:24:27.788 --> 00:24:30.982
asked the question later
as to how you get to

00:24:30.982 --> 00:24:34.270
bodhichitta?
[GMR] Maybe.

00:24:34.270 --> 00:24:36.920
[Tim] You can do that or
you can just have love

00:24:36.920 --> 00:24:38.100
and then accomplish them all.
[GMR] Yes.

00:24:38.100 --> 00:24:39.647
There's the last line there,

00:24:39.647 --> 00:24:43.230
Kirpa sarvartha sadhani.

00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:46.613
You can do those six perfections or

00:24:46.613 --> 00:24:49.460
just love people.

00:24:49.460 --> 00:24:53.847
And he kept saying that random

00:24:53.847 --> 00:24:55.600
feelings of love from people

00:24:55.600 --> 00:24:57.540
for a few minutes

00:24:57.540 --> 00:25:02.370
during the day was worth
all the six perfections,

00:25:02.370 --> 00:25:04.450
sarvartha sadhani

00:25:04.450 --> 00:25:05.820
You can reach all your goals.

00:25:05.820 --> 00:25:08.975
What's the correlation for love?

00:25:08.975 --> 00:25:12.420
Everything.

00:25:12.420 --> 00:25:13.480
Be generous, you get money.

00:25:13.480 --> 00:25:15.400
Yes, I got that.

00:25:15.400 --> 00:25:16.970
Don't hurt people, you'll be happy.

00:25:16.970 --> 00:25:18.270
Yes, I got that.

00:25:18.270 --> 00:25:19.860
Be patient, you'll be handsome.

00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:20.693
Yes, I got that.

00:25:20.693 --> 00:25:24.340
I mean, I wish I could do it.

00:25:24.340 --> 00:25:27.890
But love people even for a few minutes,

00:25:27.890 --> 00:25:29.950
then you can have everything.

00:25:29.950 --> 00:25:33.230
Then the correlation is everything.

00:25:33.230 --> 00:25:36.080
Which is the argument for bodhichitta

00:25:36.080 --> 00:25:38.590
and especially for compassion.

00:25:38.590 --> 00:25:40.565
All right, next picture.

00:25:45.556 --> 00:25:48.716
I feel uncomfortable, personally,
with the word bodhichitta

00:25:48.716 --> 00:25:51.749
being been used a lot, especially
when people misspell it

00:25:51.749 --> 00:25:54.010
with a 'c',

00:25:54.010 --> 00:25:56.470
which is not the pronunciation.

00:25:56.470 --> 00:25:58.700
It's the technical transliteration.

00:25:58.700 --> 00:26:01.010
You don't spell bodhichitta with a C.

00:26:01.010 --> 00:26:04.493
It's 'cha', it's C-H, duh.

00:26:04.493 --> 00:26:08.600
Please. Don't say bodhi-kita.

00:26:08.600 --> 00:26:10.773
Anyway, that word makes me uncomfortable

00:26:10.773 --> 00:26:14.820
because it feels so foreign.

00:26:14.820 --> 00:26:18.220
You got bodhichitta? I hope
not, I took the vaccinations.

00:26:18.220 --> 00:26:21.000
(laughing)

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:22.710
No, no, it's a good thing.

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:24.430
Yeah, well what is it?

00:26:24.430 --> 00:26:27.150
So what is it?

00:26:27.150 --> 00:26:30.330
You got to imagine

00:26:30.330 --> 00:26:34.500
sitting in a meditation
in your living room

00:26:34.500 --> 00:26:39.500
and walking between a billion
planets within 20 minutes.

00:26:39.670 --> 00:26:42.220
You got to imagine

00:26:42.220 --> 00:26:46.240
sitting on your meditation
cushion in your living room

00:26:46.240 --> 00:26:50.133
and walking on a billion
planets in reality.

00:26:52.434 --> 00:26:55.050
Really stepping from planet to planet,

00:26:55.050 --> 00:26:57.210
billions of planets,

00:26:57.210 --> 00:27:01.130
and personally meeting
each being on each planet.

00:27:01.130 --> 00:27:05.040
Each bug, each bird, each fish

00:27:05.040 --> 00:27:07.710
and promising them that
you will take care of them.

00:27:07.710 --> 00:27:10.320
You got to imagine

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:11.820
having that experience.

00:27:11.820 --> 00:27:14.260
And if you have that experience,

00:27:14.260 --> 00:27:17.760
then the rest of your life is easy.

00:27:17.760 --> 00:27:19.840
Then the rest of your life

00:27:19.840 --> 00:27:22.070
is ridiculously easy.

00:27:22.070 --> 00:27:23.987
If you just have that

00:27:23.987 --> 00:27:27.080
10, 20 minute experience,

00:27:27.080 --> 00:27:28.700
then it doesn't matter what happens

00:27:28.700 --> 00:27:33.640
for the rest of your life,
you are extraordinarily happy.

00:27:33.640 --> 00:27:35.840
And that's bodhichitta.

00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:38.800
Please don't think it's
some Buddhist weird thing.

00:27:38.800 --> 00:27:40.320
It's not that.

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:41.680
- Is it weird? - Yes.

00:27:41.680 --> 00:27:43.880
- Is it unusual? - Yes.

00:27:43.880 --> 00:27:48.238
- Is it an experience you
didn't hear about in university?

00:27:48.238 --> 00:27:49.071
- Yes.

00:27:50.330 --> 00:27:53.998
And it's the most beautiful
experience that a human being

00:27:53.998 --> 00:27:54.990
can undertake.

00:27:54.990 --> 00:27:58.010
And you will never forget it.

00:27:58.010 --> 00:28:00.810
Then your whole life will have

00:28:00.810 --> 00:28:03.288
a clear purpose.

00:28:04.407 --> 00:28:07.348
You know what you have
to do in this world.

00:28:07.348 --> 00:28:11.480
And it's very, very
relaxing and comforting.

00:28:11.480 --> 00:28:14.047
You know what your job is.

00:28:14.047 --> 00:28:17.202
Nowadays, we're restructuring
one of our companies

00:28:18.380 --> 00:28:20.560
and people are like, "What's
my job? What's my job?"

00:28:20.560 --> 00:28:22.730
I said, "Don't worry, I'm
going to write your job down."

00:28:22.730 --> 00:28:24.790
And once you write the job
down on a piece of paper

00:28:24.790 --> 00:28:26.610
and you hand it to them and
say, "This is your job."

00:28:26.610 --> 00:28:28.170
And they say, "dot, dot, dot, dot, dot"

00:28:28.170 --> 00:28:30.960
and they're like, "Ah!"

00:28:30.960 --> 00:28:32.297
At the end of the interview, I say,

00:28:32.297 --> 00:28:33.480
"Here's your business card."

00:28:33.480 --> 00:28:35.920
And they're like, "Oh man,

00:28:35.920 --> 00:28:39.120
I have a title. This is who I am.

00:28:39.120 --> 00:28:42.040
Vice President, Operations.

00:28:42.040 --> 00:28:43.280
Now I have a title".

00:28:43.280 --> 00:28:44.780
And the point is

00:28:45.960 --> 00:28:48.140
you get this bodhisattva business card

00:28:48.140 --> 00:28:52.790
that says, "Tim Lowenhaupt, Bodhisattva".

00:28:52.790 --> 00:28:54.710
Then you're like, "Ah, yeah!

00:28:54.710 --> 00:28:56.470
Now I know who I am.

00:28:56.470 --> 00:28:58.800
Now I know which company I work for.

00:28:58.800 --> 00:29:01.210
And I know what's my position.

00:29:01.210 --> 00:29:03.000
I have a position."

00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:05.170
And it feels great.

00:29:05.170 --> 00:29:07.900
That's your lifetime position.

00:29:07.900 --> 00:29:10.138
All right, next one.

00:29:11.600 --> 00:29:13.250
So please don't think about bodhichitta

00:29:13.250 --> 00:29:17.836
as some minor Buddhist
thing with a Sanskrit name.

00:29:19.546 --> 00:29:21.650
It's a lifetime experience.

00:29:21.650 --> 00:29:23.780
Once in a lifetime experience.

00:29:23.780 --> 00:29:26.076
All right. Here we go.

00:29:27.390 --> 00:29:30.500
Who's going to explain this one to me?

00:29:30.500 --> 00:29:33.510
It was a long time ago.

00:29:35.340 --> 00:29:37.730
[Tim] There's no difference between

00:29:37.730 --> 00:29:39.920
the sheep that's going to be killed today

00:29:39.920 --> 00:29:42.160
and the sheep that's
hanging out in the pasture

00:29:42.160 --> 00:29:44.657
enjoying the grass.

00:29:44.657 --> 00:29:45.490
[GMR] Yes, good, good.

00:29:45.490 --> 00:29:47.710
[Tim] They all end up the same.

00:29:47.710 --> 00:29:49.890
And it's the same here
at the Peach Tree Cafe.

00:29:49.890 --> 00:29:52.880
The people are enjoying
themselves, they're having a meal.

00:29:52.880 --> 00:29:54.370
But at the end of the day,

00:29:54.370 --> 00:29:56.075
we're all heading towards the same place,

00:29:56.075 --> 00:29:57.460
we're all in the same plane.

00:29:57.460 --> 00:29:58.523
[GMR] Yes. Cool.

00:29:58.523 --> 00:30:01.840
Each of these people,

00:30:01.840 --> 00:30:03.700
each person here

00:30:03.700 --> 00:30:06.980
is laying in a hospital bed

00:30:06.980 --> 00:30:09.700
taking their last few breaths after

00:30:09.700 --> 00:30:11.460
a failed fight with cancer.

00:30:11.460 --> 00:30:13.017
I always think it's so funny they say,

00:30:13.017 --> 00:30:14.800
"After a brave battle with cancer."

00:30:14.800 --> 00:30:19.800
Come on, battles with
cancer are always one sided.

00:30:19.800 --> 00:30:21.525
You die.

00:30:22.950 --> 00:30:24.960
So each person here,

00:30:24.960 --> 00:30:28.080
who's eating the famous cinnamon roll

00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:29.860
at the Peach Tree Cafe,

00:30:29.860 --> 00:30:33.400
they're as good as a
person dying in a hospital.

00:30:33.400 --> 00:30:35.950
They just look different.

00:30:37.400 --> 00:30:40.250
Then don't forget, they have been our mom.

00:30:40.250 --> 00:30:41.700
So we have to take care of them.

00:30:41.700 --> 00:30:43.820
We should try to help them.

00:30:43.820 --> 00:30:45.693
All right, next picture.

00:30:47.230 --> 00:30:49.090
Somebody tell me

00:30:49.090 --> 00:30:52.885
why this picture is the
same as the last picture.

00:30:54.159 --> 00:30:56.009
[Gyelse] Because they're young people,

00:30:56.009 --> 00:30:58.539
they're vibrant, and alive, and everything

00:30:58.539 --> 00:31:01.633
is going for them, but
they're also going to die.

00:31:01.633 --> 00:31:04.160
[GMR] Yes. There're also laying in a bed

00:31:04.160 --> 00:31:07.150
in the hospital room
breathing their last breath

00:31:07.150 --> 00:31:09.713
except they have a worst disadvantage.

00:31:12.300 --> 00:31:14.217
The excitement of life.

00:31:18.380 --> 00:31:20.740
They're doing small bad things

00:31:20.740 --> 00:31:23.990
to get this new house
and to get this nice job

00:31:23.990 --> 00:31:27.490
and to buy that nice
shirt and that nice dress.

00:31:27.490 --> 00:31:30.250
They're not cruel people.

00:31:30.250 --> 00:31:33.350
They didn't put people in
the gas chamber or something,

00:31:33.350 --> 00:31:34.630
they're not like that.

00:31:34.630 --> 00:31:36.860
They are normal people.

00:31:36.860 --> 00:31:39.120
They are doing small

00:31:39.120 --> 00:31:42.110
things to ignore other people

00:31:42.110 --> 00:31:43.500
and to hurt other people.

00:31:43.500 --> 00:31:46.820
They're not bad people, but
they are constantly collecting

00:31:46.820 --> 00:31:48.510
small seeds

00:31:48.510 --> 00:31:52.070
that will multiply and bring
them huge suffering later.

00:31:52.070 --> 00:31:55.140
These people are suffering as much

00:31:55.140 --> 00:31:57.760
as a 90 year old woman

00:31:57.760 --> 00:32:01.470
on the resuscitator,

00:32:01.470 --> 00:32:03.910
on oxygen in the hospital, dying.

00:32:03.910 --> 00:32:05.100
These people are the same.

00:32:05.100 --> 00:32:07.340
In fact, these people are worse

00:32:07.340 --> 00:32:10.331
because they're collecting karma to be

00:32:10.331 --> 00:32:13.170
in that hospital bed 1,000 times,

00:32:13.170 --> 00:32:15.373
in this real estate interview,

00:32:16.720 --> 00:32:18.890
during this real estate interview.

00:32:20.100 --> 00:32:21.868
All right, next.

00:32:22.880 --> 00:32:25.161
Got two more to go.

00:32:26.320 --> 00:32:27.760
And this is called TSAD,

00:32:27.760 --> 00:32:30.642
and Tim's going to explain it, TSAD.

00:32:33.310 --> 00:32:35.950
Talk louder, talk louder.

00:32:35.950 --> 00:32:39.420
[im] She's at home having
breakfast, but her daughter,

00:32:39.420 --> 00:32:43.050
her baby's in the hospital, sick.

00:32:43.050 --> 00:32:45.887
And she can't think about anything else

00:32:45.887 --> 00:32:48.180
but her sick daughter.

00:32:48.180 --> 00:32:51.890
And so she is... TSAD means to measure.

00:32:51.890 --> 00:32:53.760
She knows that she has something

00:32:53.760 --> 00:32:56.007
but I don't remember what it is right now.

00:32:56.007 --> 00:32:58.590
[GMR] How do we know when we got to-

00:32:58.590 --> 00:33:00.760
[Tim] How do we know when we have love?

00:33:00.760 --> 00:33:02.400
[GMR] Compassion.

00:33:02.400 --> 00:33:04.010
How do we know?

00:33:04.010 --> 00:33:07.058
Okay, if I ask you,

00:33:08.842 --> 00:33:12.540
what did Pabongka Rinpoche say

00:33:12.540 --> 00:33:14.340
was the sign

00:33:14.340 --> 00:33:16.180
that you have reached the goal

00:33:16.180 --> 00:33:18.745
of developing compassion?

00:33:19.770 --> 00:33:21.913
[Tim] Personal responsibility.

00:33:22.930 --> 00:33:24.010
[GMR] Or even while you're-

00:33:24.010 --> 00:33:25.520
[Paulina] You only think about it.

00:33:25.520 --> 00:33:27.620
[GMR] Yes, you can't
stop thinking about it.

00:33:27.620 --> 00:33:29.640
Mainly it's that. You can't
stop thinking about it.

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:33.510
Any more than a mom could stop
thinking about her daughter

00:33:33.510 --> 00:33:36.190
who's in the hospital
after a bad car accident

00:33:36.190 --> 00:33:37.700
the night before.

00:33:37.700 --> 00:33:40.620
I have a friend who had a lung operation

00:33:40.620 --> 00:33:42.890
in the last week or two.

00:33:42.890 --> 00:33:46.367
They cut out half of one of his lungs.

00:33:46.367 --> 00:33:49.970
He's young. He's very successful.

00:33:49.970 --> 00:33:53.060
He's handsome. He's kind.

00:33:53.060 --> 00:33:55.003
He has two beautiful kids.

00:33:56.350 --> 00:33:58.730
And to talk to his wife

00:33:58.730 --> 00:34:02.243
during that day that he was
getting his lung cut out,

00:34:04.810 --> 00:34:06.640
she wasn't thinking about anything else.

00:34:06.640 --> 00:34:08.210
She wasn't like, "Geshe-la

00:34:08.210 --> 00:34:10.950
how's the weather in Sedona?"

00:34:10.950 --> 00:34:13.730
She's only focused on one thing.

00:34:13.730 --> 00:34:15.890
That's the measure -- TSAD --

00:34:15.890 --> 00:34:17.720
that you have compassion.

00:34:17.720 --> 00:34:19.330
When you're thinking
like that all the time.

00:34:19.330 --> 00:34:22.110
I don't know if you can
enjoy coffee, probably.

00:34:22.110 --> 00:34:25.090
If you're super

00:34:25.090 --> 00:34:27.910
meditator you can have
coffee and worry about people

00:34:27.910 --> 00:34:29.570
at the same time.

00:34:29.570 --> 00:34:31.070
Okay, we got one more to go.

00:34:31.070 --> 00:34:33.633
And Pachi offered to explain it.

00:34:33.633 --> 00:34:35.060
(students laughing)

00:34:35.060 --> 00:34:36.430
Loud, okay?

00:34:36.430 --> 00:34:41.000
[Pachi] She's taking
personal responsibility.

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:42.616
[GMR] Yes.

00:34:44.010 --> 00:34:45.870
By the way, usually when they explain it,

00:34:45.870 --> 00:34:48.400
they do it this way.

00:34:48.400 --> 00:34:49.233
Look,

00:34:51.250 --> 00:34:53.330
I'm going to take responsibility

00:34:53.330 --> 00:34:56.160
that plastic doesn't spread in the world.

00:34:56.160 --> 00:34:58.270
Me and V were

00:34:58.270 --> 00:35:00.600
eating tsampa this morning

00:35:00.600 --> 00:35:03.330
and we got it from Tibet.

00:35:03.330 --> 00:35:05.280
And it comes in a cloth bag

00:35:05.280 --> 00:35:07.970
and V was like, "Wow, we could wash this

00:35:07.970 --> 00:35:08.803
and use it again."

00:35:08.803 --> 00:35:12.296
I'm like, "Duh."

00:35:12.296 --> 00:35:14.750
And then she said, "Probably
people used to do that."

00:35:14.750 --> 00:35:16.940
I'm like, "Yeah, I guess so."

00:35:16.940 --> 00:35:18.890
You just wash the bag again

00:35:18.890 --> 00:35:20.600
and then you put more tsampa in it.

00:35:20.600 --> 00:35:24.610
So if you decide, I won't use plastic.

00:35:24.610 --> 00:35:27.180
We were talking about our friend Añjali.

00:35:27.180 --> 00:35:31.770
She decided to be careful with packaging.

00:35:31.770 --> 00:35:35.673
She puts all her garbage in
a box like this every week.

00:35:36.930 --> 00:35:39.880
We have 24 bags of stuff

00:35:39.880 --> 00:35:43.283
and if we just decide, each person,

00:35:44.290 --> 00:35:47.013
I don't know what the rest
of the world is going to do.

00:35:48.794 --> 00:35:50.780
I know when they're
driving these big trucks

00:35:50.780 --> 00:35:53.470
and using gas they don't have to use.

00:35:53.470 --> 00:35:56.670
I know they're throwing
away all kinds of plastic

00:35:56.670 --> 00:35:57.813
every moment.

00:35:59.270 --> 00:36:03.010
But I personally have made a decision

00:36:03.010 --> 00:36:07.050
that I will pick up garbage
than I see on the road.

00:36:07.050 --> 00:36:11.410
Or I will be the one who
picks up the garbage quietly

00:36:11.410 --> 00:36:13.680
and throws it in the garbage can

00:36:13.680 --> 00:36:14.980
for everybody else.

00:36:14.980 --> 00:36:18.090
And that's a quiet,

00:36:18.090 --> 00:36:20.030
modest,

00:36:20.030 --> 00:36:23.380
personal decision that you make

00:36:23.380 --> 00:36:27.050
to serve other people,
even if they don't know

00:36:27.050 --> 00:36:29.630
and serve future generations.

00:36:29.630 --> 00:36:31.300
I will take care.

00:36:31.300 --> 00:36:33.860
I'll be the one, I'll start.

00:36:33.860 --> 00:36:38.059
I'll take care of future generations.

00:36:38.059 --> 00:36:41.483
And I will take care of this world.

00:36:43.050 --> 00:36:46.300
I will do my little part
to pass this world on

00:36:46.300 --> 00:36:49.210
to the next generation a little cleaner.

00:36:49.210 --> 00:36:51.860
Or some kind of personal
responsibility like that.

00:36:51.860 --> 00:36:54.940
So that's the sixth step

00:36:54.940 --> 00:36:58.920
of developing bodhichitta
and then next course

00:36:58.920 --> 00:37:02.470
we're going to go into
what does it feel like

00:37:02.470 --> 00:37:03.810
when you hit #7,

00:37:03.810 --> 00:37:07.620
which is a famous result of the first six.

00:37:07.620 --> 00:37:10.570
So next course, we'll be going into

00:37:12.040 --> 00:37:14.270
what does it feel like when you get there?

00:37:14.270 --> 00:37:16.337
Okay. I'd like to thank Tim Lowenhaupt

00:37:16.337 --> 00:37:18.403
for working so hard.

00:37:21.510 --> 00:37:23.943
He works all day, all night.

00:37:26.727 --> 00:37:31.727
His work to run these hotels in this town,

00:37:32.250 --> 00:37:35.190
pays my salary, thank you for that.

00:37:35.190 --> 00:37:39.030
And a lot of other people's livelihood.

00:37:39.030 --> 00:37:40.930
And because of him,

00:37:40.930 --> 00:37:44.890
I have a refrigerator, et cetera.

00:37:44.890 --> 00:37:48.451
Then he also took responsibility

00:37:48.451 --> 00:37:51.570
to produce these Lam Rims.

00:37:51.570 --> 00:37:55.060
And I think the greatest
achievement he did

00:37:55.060 --> 00:37:57.290
was that he attracted,

00:37:57.290 --> 00:38:00.780
he is a nice person,

00:38:00.780 --> 00:38:04.740
and he has attracted many, many
volunteers around the world

00:38:04.740 --> 00:38:07.150
to help

00:38:07.150 --> 00:38:08.730
because he's such a nice person.

00:38:08.730 --> 00:38:10.490
So thank you for that.

00:38:10.490 --> 00:38:12.550
And thank you, Pachi and Pau

00:38:12.550 --> 00:38:15.660
for flying up from Mexico
to run all this equipment

00:38:15.660 --> 00:38:18.400
because grandpa in 1,000 years

00:38:18.400 --> 00:38:21.542
could never run this equipment.

00:38:21.542 --> 00:38:25.182
Venerable Gyalse thank
you for Diamond Mountain.

00:38:25.182 --> 00:38:28.700
Venerable Connie thank you for YSI

00:38:28.700 --> 00:38:30.640
and keeping us healthy

00:38:30.640 --> 00:38:31.860
and keeping us going.

00:38:31.860 --> 00:38:33.670
And Christina Kasica,

00:38:33.670 --> 00:38:36.400
thank you for quietly, anonymously

00:38:36.400 --> 00:38:38.870
taking care of all our needs.

00:38:38.870 --> 00:38:42.360
It's very graceful and very
elegant and thank you for that.

00:38:42.360 --> 00:38:44.250
Okay. And thank you Venerable Jigme

00:38:44.250 --> 00:38:45.930
for driving me around

00:38:45.930 --> 00:38:48.620
so I can get the Lam Rim done in the car.

00:38:48.620 --> 00:38:51.550
See you guys at the next Lam Rim. Yay!

